     <article class="story single" data-beat="funny">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FUNNY</span> · r/nottheonion · 2026-09-29 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>$745K Returned After GoFundMe Fraud</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#funny</span> <span>#scam</span> <span>#gofundme</span> <span>#true-story</span></div>
       <p>A GoFundMe campaign has resulted in the return of $745,000 to donors after it was discovered that the daughter of the elderly couple featured in the story had fabricated the identity of a neighbor. The campaign, which had raised substantial funds for the couple, was shut down when the discrepancy in the narrative came to light. The daughter’s fabrication of a neighbor’s identity raised immediate red flags, leading to an investigation by the platform. The sum of $745,000 is a significant amount, highlighting the scale of the fundraising effort and the trust placed by donors. The incident serves as a stark reminder of the risks associated with online fundraising, where verifying the details of a story can be challenging. The fact that the daughter was involved in the deception adds a layer of complexity to the case. The funds have now been returned to the original donors, a process that underscores the platform’s commitment to accountability. The elderly couple, presumably unaware of the extent of the fraud, are likely left to deal with the aftermath of their daughter’s actions. The story is a cautionary tale for both donors and fundraisers, emphasizing the need for transparency and verification. The return of the funds is a positive step, but the damage to trust remains. The incident highlights the importance of due diligence in charitable giving.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-29/745k-returned-after-gofundme-fraud.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Kicker: The neighbor was real only on the internet.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately reflects the source's core narrative regarding the GoFundMe fraud and the return of funds.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/nottheonion/comments/1wsyc8n/745k_returned_to_donors_after_gofundme_says/" rel="noopener">r/nottheonion</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
