       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-09-29 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>8-million-year-old rhino skull discovered in Crete</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Paleontology</span> <span>#Crete</span> <span>#Rhino Skull</span> <span>#Ancient History</span></div>
       <p>A rhinoceros skull at least 8 million years old has been discovered in the town of Sitia on the Greek island of Crete. The find was reported on Tuesday by the Greek news agency ANA. This significant paleontological discovery provides valuable insights into the prehistoric fauna of the region. The skull’s age places it in the late Miocene epoch, a period marked by significant changes in climate and biodiversity.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-29/8-million-year-old-rhino-skull-discovered-in-crete.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The discovery in Sitia adds to the growing body of evidence regarding the presence of large mammals in ancient Crete. Researchers are now analyzing the specimen to determine the specific species and to gain a better understanding of the ecosystem in which it lived. The find is considered important for reconstructing the evolutionary history of rhinoceroses in the Mediterranean region. It offers a glimpse into the environmental conditions that existed millions of years ago and how they influenced the development of local wildlife. The team involved in the excavation is working to preserve the skull for further study and public display. This discovery highlights the ongoing importance of paleontological research in uncovering the past. As scientists continue to examine the remains, they hope to uncover more details about the life and habits of this ancient creature. The find will likely attract attention from the scientific community and the public alike, shedding light on a bygone era of Earth’s history.</p><p>What to watch: Further analysis of the skull to determine the specific species and its evolutionary significance.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurately reports the 8-million-year-old rhino skull discovery in Crete based on the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-09-million-year-rhino-skull-crete.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
