     <article class="story single" data-beat="entertainment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENTERTAINMENT</span> · Variety · 2026-09-29 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Connected TV Reshaping Indian Storytelling, Say Media Chiefs</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Connected TV</span> <span>#Indian Media</span> <span>#Prime Video</span> <span>#FICCI-Frames</span></div>
       <p>Connected TV is profoundly transforming Indian media, impacting not just how audiences watch content but also the very nature of what companies produce. This significant shift was a key discussion point among executives from Prime Video, Sony, JioStar, Zee, and NDTV at the FICCI-Frames conference in Mumbai. Industry leaders noted that the advent of Connected TV is prompting a re-evaluation of content development strategies across the board.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-29/connected-tv-reshaping-indian-storytelling-say-media-chiefs.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Prime Video, for instance, is now actively developing shows with an eye toward whether audiences will engage with them collectively. This marks a notable evolution from the early days of streaming, which were predominantly characterized by largely solo viewing experiences. Executives emphasized that the move towards considering shared viewership during content creation reflects a deeper understanding of evolving audience habits and the capabilities of modern platforms.</p><p>Gaurav Gandhi, Vice President and Head of Prime Video for Asia-Pacific and Australia-New, contributed to these discussions, highlighting the strategic adjustments being made. The collective sentiment at the conference pointed to a future where content is increasingly tailored for diverse viewing environments, including those that foster communal engagement. This adaptation is seen as crucial for remaining relevant in a dynamic media landscape.</p><p>The changing dynamics underscore how technology is driving fundamental changes in creative processes and business models within the Indian entertainment sector, pushing for innovative approaches to storytelling.</p><p>What to watch: The future of Indian content creation.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately reflects the source summary and expands on the context without inventing new facts.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://variety.com/2026/tv/news/prime-video-sony-jiostar-chiefs-connected-tv-storytelling-1236878159/" rel="noopener">Variety</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
