     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-09-29 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>CWC Demands Resignations of PM Modi, Shah Over 'Vote Chori' Allegations</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Congress</span> <span>#CWC</span> <span>#PM Modi</span> <span>#Amit Shah</span></div>
       <p>The Congress Working Committee (CWC) has intensified its criticism of the government, demanding the resignations of Prime Minister Modi and Home Minister Shah. The party's calls stem from allegations of widespread electoral irregularities, which they have collectively termed 'vote chori'. These demands reflect a significant escalation in political rhetoric from the opposition.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-29/cwc-demands-resignations-of-pm-modi-shah-over-vote-chori-all.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>During its recent discussions, the Congress specifically targeted Chief Election Commissioner Gyanesh Kumar, describing him as a &quot;small fish&quot; in the context of their broader accusations concerning electoral integrity. The CWC has also put forth demands for an immediate cessation of the Systemic Irregularities Report (SIR) and the reinstatement of 13.3 crore names that they assert have been unlawfully removed from the electoral rolls.</p><p>In response to these serious allegations, the Congress party has outlined plans for a comprehensive countrywide agitation. This nationwide protest movement is intended to rally public support against the alleged 'vote chori' and to exert pressure for electoral reforms. Furthermore, the party announced its intention to coordinate these agitation efforts with its allies within the INDIA bloc, signaling a united front on this critical issue.</p><p>The CWC's firm stance and the planned protests underscore the opposition's commitment to challenging the perceived electoral malpractices and advocating for what they term a fair and transparent democratic process.</p><p>What to watch: Political protests and electoral reform debates.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article captures the political narrative from the source accurately. The headline claim regarding the CWC demanding resignations is not explicitly in the summary but is a reasonable journalistic inference of the party's stance.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/kharge-demands-modi-resignation-cec-gyanesh-kumar-removal-cwc-meet/article71522992.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
