     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · Livemint Money · 2026-09-29 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Homebuyers Can Claim Interest on Delayed Flats Even After Accepting Possession</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Real Estate</span> <span>#RERA</span> <span>#Homebuyers</span> <span>#Allahabad High Court</span></div>
       <p>For homebuyers in India who have accepted possession of a delayed flat, recent legal developments offer significant relief. The Allahabad High Court has clarified that accepting possession of a property does not automatically bar a buyer from claiming interest on the delayed period. This ruling is a crucial update for ordinary savers and taxpayers who have invested in real estate.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-29/homebuyers-can-claim-interest-on-delayed-flats-even-after-ac.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Previously, many buyers might have believed that once they took possession of their flat, any claim for compensation or interest regarding the delay was forfeited. However, the court has stated that this is not the case. The ruling emphasizes that the right to claim interest for the delay remains intact, even after the physical handover of the property.</p><p>Furthermore, the court noted that RERA (Real Estate Regulatory Authority) rules do not specify a limit period for such payments. This means there is no strict deadline within which a buyer must file their claim for interest, providing a wider window for homeowners to seek redressal.</p><p>This decision simplifies the process for homebuyers, allowing them to pursue financial compensation for the inconvenience and financial strain caused by construction delays. It places the onus on developers to account for these delays and compensate buyers accordingly.</p><p>For the average taxpayer, this means greater protection and transparency in real estate transactions. It ensures that buyers are not left bearing the financial burden of delays without recourse.</p><p>What to watch: How developers and the RERA implement this ruling in practical terms for ongoing and future projects.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately interprets the legal ruling described in the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.livemint.com/money/homebuyers-continue-interest-claim-delayed-flat-after-accept-possession-allahabad-hc-rules-dont-lose-right-no-rera-limit-11790702301893.html" rel="noopener">Livemint Money</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
