       <h1>Indian Students Pursuing MBBS in Georgia Urged to Verify Licensing Rules</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Education</span> <span>#Careers</span> <span>#MBBS</span> <span>#Georgia</span></div>
       <p>Indian students considering medical education overseas, particularly those eyeing MBBS programs in Georgia, are strongly advised to exercise caution and thoroughly review the licensing regulations governing medical practice in the country. The Education Times emphasizes the critical need for prospective students to carefully ascertain the specific requirements and protocols. This due diligence is essential to ensure that their qualifications will be recognized and valid upon their return to India or for practice in other jurisdictions. Without proper verification of licensing rules, students may encounter significant hurdles in their career progression after completing their studies. The summary highlights that students need to proactively investigate all regulatory aspects before committing to an overseas medical degree. This includes understanding the process for obtaining a license to practice, any reciprocity agreements, and the criteria for eligibility. While specific dates, detailed eligibility criteria, or official links are not provided in the available summary, the overarching message underscores the paramount importance of individual research into the regulatory landscape. A careful and informed approach will help prevent future complications related to professional accreditation and practice rights, ensuring that their investment in education leads to a viable career.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-29/indian-students-pursuing-mbbs-in-georgia-urged-to-verify-lic.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>What to watch: Future updates on specific licensing requirements for Indian medical graduates from Georgia.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately reflects the source's warning to students regarding Georgian medical licensing.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://news.google.?oc=5" rel="noopener">Education (aggregate)</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
