     <article class="story single" data-beat="education">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">EDUCATION</span> · Education (aggregate) · 2026-09-29 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>New NCERT Social Science text replaces European revolutions with resistance movements in medieval India - The Hindu</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#NCERT</span> <span>#Social Science</span> <span>#Curriculum Change</span> <span>#Indian History</span></div>
       <p>A significant revision in the National Council of Educational Research and Training (NCERT) Social Science textbooks has been reported by The Hindu. The new curriculum updates include a notable change in the historical content presented to students. Specifically, the revised text now features resistance movements in medieval India, replacing previously covered topics on European revolutions.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-29/new-ncert-social-science-text-replaces-european-revolutions-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>This alteration indicates a shift in the pedagogical approach and historical emphasis within the Indian education system. By substituting European historical events with narratives from medieval Indian resistance, the NCERT appears to be re-contextualizing the historical perspectives offered to students. This move could aim to provide a more India-centric view of historical struggles and movements, aligning the curriculum more closely with national narratives.</p><p>The summary, however, does not provide specific details regarding the exact dates of implementation for these new textbooks, eligibility criteria for any related exams, information on results, admissions, job opportunities, or official links to the NCERT website for further details. The report from The Hindu primarily highlights the thematic change in the Social Science curriculum. This change is likely to spark discussions among educators, historians, and policymakers about the broader implications for historical education in India.</p><p>What to watch: Debates and analyses regarding the new NCERT curriculum changes.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately reflects the information provided in the source summary.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://news.google.?oc=5" rel="noopener">Education (aggregate)</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
