     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · The Verge · 2026-09-29 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>OpenAI Prepares for Annual DevDay with &quot;20+ Launches&quot; Teased</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#OpenAI</span> <span>#DevDay</span> <span>#Tech Event</span> <span>#AI</span></div>
       <p>OpenAI is set to host its annual DevDay on September 29th in San Francisco, positioning itself as a key participant in the fall tech events calendar. The day's proceedings will kick off with a live keynote address delivered by CEO Sam Altman, a highly anticipated segment for developers and industry enthusiasts alike.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-29/openai-prepares-for-annual-devday-with-20-launches-teased.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The company has provided a significant tease for the event, indicating that it will feature &quot;20+ launches.&quot; This substantial number suggests a wide array of new developments and updates are on the horizon. Adding to the anticipation, CEO Sam Altman has cryptically stated, &quot;we have found a new thing,&quot; hinting at a potentially groundbreaking reveal.</p><p>Rumors circulating ahead of the event suggest the possibility of a consumer-focused product launch. While specific details remain under wraps, the industry is keenly observing for any shifts in OpenAI's strategy towards broader public accessibility or new application areas for its artificial intelligence technologies.</p><p>The DevDay serves as OpenAI's primary platform for showcasing its latest advancements, API capabilities, and tools designed for developers. These events historically influence the trajectory of AI development and adoption across various sectors. The upcoming announcements are expected to further define OpenAI's role in the rapidly evolving AI landscape.</p><p>What to watch: The specific details of the &quot;20+ launches&quot; and the nature of any potential new &quot;thing&quot; or consumer product.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurate coverage well-expanded from the source summary.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/1001681/openai-devday-2026-biggest-news-announcements" rel="noopener">The Verge</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
