     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-09-29 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Scientists call for protection and restoration of Africa's seagrass meadows</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Seagrass</span> <span>#Africa</span> <span>#Restoration</span> <span>#Marine Biology</span></div>
       <p>A team of African researchers has published the first comprehensive review of seagrass restoration activities across the continent's coastline. This landmark study aims to highlight the importance of seagrass meadows and calls for their protection and restoration. Seagrass meadows are vital ecosystems that provide numerous environmental benefits, including carbon sequestration, coastal protection, and habitat for marine life. The review synthesizes existing efforts and identifies gaps in current restoration practices.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-29/scientists-call-for-protection-and-restoration-of-africas-se.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The researchers emphasize the need for a coordinated approach to preserving these critical habitats. By documenting the state of seagrass restoration across Africa, the study provides a baseline for future conservation strategies. The findings suggest that while some progress has been made, there is a significant need for increased investment and scientific support. The team advocates for policies that prioritize the protection of remaining meadows and the active restoration of degraded areas. This initiative is part of a broader global effort to combat climate change and restore marine biodiversity. The review serves as a resource for policymakers, conservationists, and local communities involved in coastal management. It underscores the role of seagrass in maintaining ecological balance and supporting sustainable livelihoods. As coastal pressures continue to rise, the urgency of protecting these ecosystems becomes increasingly apparent. The study offers a roadmap for enhancing restoration efforts and ensuring the long-term viability of seagrass meadows in Africa.</p><p>What to watch: The implementation of new conservation policies based on the review’s recommendations.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Great expansion on the African researchers' review of seagrass restoration.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-09-scientists-africa-seagrass-meadows.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
