     <article class="story single" data-beat="positive">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">POSITIVE</span> · The Better India · 2026-09-29 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Shifting Periods from Panic to Preparation</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#health</span> <span>#education</span> <span>#menstruation</span></div>
       <p>For many girls, the first period arrives with confusion and fear, often marked by a sudden stain and a question: &quot;Am I okay?&quot; The reaction of adults in that moment shapes the child’s long-term relationship with her body. A calm reassurance that menstruation is normal can transform a frightening experience into an ordinary part of growing up. Dr. Neha Abhijit Pawar, a gynaecologist in Mumbai, advises parents to begin these conversations around age eight or nine, using simple language rather than a single, intimidating lecture. By introducing the topic gradually alongside other body changes, parents can prevent the first period from feeling like an injury. Practical preparation is also key. Creating a small kit with pads, spare underwear, and tissues, and identifying a trusted adult at school, empowers children to handle the moment with confidence. When a child asks, &quot;Why am I bleeding?&quot; the priority is reassurance, not a biology lesson. Teaching girls to separate facts from myths, such as the idea that period blood is dirty, helps them understand their bodies without shame. The goal is not just to explain the cycle, but to ensure that when the moment comes, nothing feels unfamiliar. With open, ongoing dialogue, girls can grow up knowing that menstruation is not something to hide, but a natural process they can manage with dignity and self-knowledge.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-29/shifting-periods-from-panic-to-preparation.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Why it matters: Early, calm education replaces panic with confidence, ensuring girls view menstruation as a normal part of life rather than a source of shame.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately summarizes the source material, capturing the core advice and themes provided by the expert.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://thebetterindia.com/parenting/how-to-prepare-daughter-first-period-gynaecologist-guide-12584274" rel="noopener">The Better India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
