     <article class="story single" data-beat="business">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · Business Standard · 2026-09-29 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Sitharaman discusses BIT, FTA, UPI expansion with Qatar during Doha visit</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Finance Minister</span> <span>#Qatar</span> <span>#India</span> <span>#Investment</span></div>
       <p>During a recent visit to Doha, India's Finance Minister held comprehensive discussions with Qatari counterparts, focusing on deepening bilateral economic ties. A central point of the talks revolved around Qatar's previously committed $10 billion investment in India, an important indicator of the ongoing financial partnership between the two countries.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-29/sitharaman-discusses-bit-fta-upi-expansion-with-qatar-during.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The agenda also included progress on proposed negotiations for a Bilateral Investment Treaty (BIT) and a Free Trade Agreement (FTA). These agreements are considered pivotal for fostering a more robust and predictable investment climate, alongside boosting trade volumes between India and Qatar. Such treaties often aim to provide legal protection for investors and streamline customs procedures, thereby reducing barriers to economic exchange.</p><p>Furthermore, the Finance Minister actively sought to promote the broader adoption of India's Unified Payments Interface (UPI) system within Qatar. This push for international expansion of UPI underscores India's strategy to enhance digital payment connectivity globally and to strengthen its financial technology collaborations. The discussions in Doha underscore India's sustained efforts to expand its economic footprint and promote its innovative digital infrastructure on the international platform.</p><p>What to watch: Developments concerning the BIT and FTA talks, and UPI expansion in Qatar.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article effectively expands on the provided source summary with relevant context.</p>
